<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jani Beg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1357</span> · Also known as Jani Beg Khan, Janibeg, Djanibek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jani Beg was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from 1342 to 1357, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan">Uzbek Khan</a>. During his siege of the Genoese port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in 1346 plague broke out among his army, and ships from Caffa may have carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1357 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and executed the Chupanid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ashraf">Malik Ashraf</a>, but he died soon after, and the Horde fell into two decades of civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jani-beg.jpg" alt="Jani Beg" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1405-1409 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jani_Beg_Khan_enthroned,_in_Timurid_genealogy_1405-1409_(Topkapi_Sarayi_M%C3%BCzesi,_H2152)_f.38b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
